    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s13753-026-00702-4",
    "abstract": "Abstract Although the human health risk from cold stress may be greater than from heat stress, population cold exposure has received little attention compared to heat exposure in the context of climate warming. A particular puzzle is that while the number of cold days has markedly decreased under climate warming, the cold-related influenza positivity rate has substantially increased. Here we reveal global hourly population exposure for different cold categories based on observations and climate model simulations from 1979 to 2100, and explore the potential link between population cold exposure and influenza positivity rate. Our results show that the number of cold hours did not decrease uniformly across all categories under climate warming, but shifted from extreme cold to moderate cold. Global hourly population cold exposure increased substantially from 1979 to 2023 (2.05 \u00d7 10 10 person-hours yr \u22121 ), and this trend is expected to persist in the near term with continued population growth. The number of influenza positives and the influenza positivity rate were significantly correlated with hourly population cold exposure. These findings imply a current risk of population cold exposure and emphasize the need for increased attention to this risk.",
    "field": "Climate change impacts on human health",
    "problem": "The paper addresses the puzzle of why cold-related influenza positivity has substantially increased under climate warming, even though the number of cold days has decreased, a risk that has received less attention than heat stress.",
    "method": "The study quantifies global hourly population exposure to different cold categories by integrating hourly climate data with population data to analyze trends, attribute causes, and explore the link between cold exposure and influenza positivity rates.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify historical and future global hourly population exposure to different categories of cold from 1979 to 2100, and to explore the potential link between this exposure and influenza positivity rates.",
    "model": [
        "GFDL-ESM4",
        "MIROC-ES2L"
    ],
